+# What owns the post-approval tail: ready-queue selection, not review execution
+
+**Class:** research inquiry and reform ideas for legislative consideration.
+**NOT** a bill, sponsorship, ballot, law change, compliance finding, Founder
+communication, implementation or verification claim. All measurement is
+read-only over existing state files; no government script, receipt, appointment
+or live state was modified.
+**Date:** 2026-11-25T13:35Z. **Author:** Executive Innovation Office (Act 25).
+
+## Relation to prior work (preserved, not overwritten)
+
+`2026-11-25-post-approval-landing-tail.md` (landed on main as `e6710487`)
+corrected an earlier latency note by splitting the commission→landing interval
+at the recorded approval and showed that review work is fast while the tail is
+**post-approval**. It offered P1 (per-attempt reason codes), P2 (hot-ledger
+append discipline), P3 (reconsider reviewer-pool/timeouts) and P4 (status quo),
+and asked Parliament which to prioritise.
+
+This note answers part of that question with **per-attempt attribution**. It
+does not replace the prior note; it adds evidence that narrows P1 and weakens
+one of the prior note's *mechanism* guesses (canonical-preflight dirty-file
+waits), while strengthening P1's core case. All figures in the prior note remain
+as recorded at its measurement time.
+
+## 1. Question
+
+The prior note could show *where* the tail lives but not *why* an approved
+candidate waits. Two candidate mechanisms were named there without attribution:
+moving-main candidate rebuild and `canonical_preflight` "edited/untracked file
+would be overwritten" waits; a DNS outage was observed in `run.log` but not
+attributed to specific attempts. This study asks: in the concrete >1 h
+post-approval cases, what does the durable integrator log actually show, and is
+the wait explainable from recorded state?
+
+## 2. Method (reproducible, read-only)
+
+Sources, all under `/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-state/delivery/`:
+
+- `integrator/state.json` — per-attempt `proposed_at`,
+  `review_commissioned_at`, `landed_at`, `status`, `office`.
+- `reviews.jsonl` — `branch`, `head`, `verdict: approve`, `at`.
+- `integrator/run.log` — timestamped integrator events.
+
+Join: a landed attempt is included only if `reviews.jsonl` has an `approve` on
+the **exact** `(branch, head)`; the earliest approve is used. Wall-clock
+durations are epoch differences; no negative intervals were found. Signals are
+substring matches on `run.log` lines whose timestamp lies inside
+`[approve_at, landed_at]`:
+
+- `fetch_dns` — "fetch failed" / "Temporary failure in name resolution";
+- `lock_busy` — "internal lock busy" / "owns worktree";
+- `commissioned_review` — "commissioned reviewer";
+- `landed` — a landing event;
+- `preflight_wait`, `suite_status`, `conflict`, `blocked` — the mechanisms
+  hypothesised previously.
+
+**Snapshot:** measurement at 2026-11-25T13:35Z gives **n = 157** reviewed
+landings (the prior note measured n = 133 earlier the same day; the population
+grew with subsequent landings). Script and raw join retained at
+`/tmp/opencode/attribute.py` and `/tmp/opencode/postapproval.json` for the
+duration of this box.
+
+## 3. Findings
+
+### 3.1 The split still holds
+
+| stage | median | p90 | max |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| review work (commission → recorded approval) | **1.9 min** | 10.1 min | 25.3 min |
+| post-approval landing (approval → landing) | 5.7 min | **70.0 min** | **1216.8 min (20.3 h)** |
+
+No review in the retained window took over 26 minutes. `n` differs from the
+prior note; medians/maxima are consistent with it.
+
+### 3.2 Signal presence in the 19 cases with post-approval > 1 h
+
+| signal | cases containing it | total matching lines |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| `landed` (other deliveries landing during the wait) | 17 / 19 | 152 |
+| `commissioned_review` (new reviews commissioned during the wait) | 17 / 19 | 143 |
+| `lock_busy` | 15 / 19 | 53 |
+| `fetch_dns` | 2 / 19 | 24 |
+| `preflight_wait` | 0 / 19 | 0 |
+| `suite_status` | 0 / 19 | 0 |
+| `conflict` / `blocked` | 0 / 19 | 0 |
+
+The integrator was **not asleep** during these waits: in 17 of 19 cases it was
+actively commissioning reviews and landing other deliveries. The dominant
+observable pattern is that an already-approved candidate sits in a ready set
+while *other* work proceeds. This is a **selection/ordering** phenomenon, not a
+review-duration phenomenon.
+
+### 3.3 The 20.3 h worst case, decomposed
+
+`p1795374202-deterministic-sources-preregister` (`executive`), head
+`3b04bf43d805`:
+
+- Approved and durable in `reviews.jsonl` at **2026-11-23T12:25:46Z**.
+- The reviewer agent had finished in ~51 s (prior note, §3.2).
+- Between approval and landing the log records **19 reviewer commissions and
+  44 landing events**, plus a DNS outage with **24** `fetch failed` lines from
+  17:44Z to ~19:03Z. There are **48** further log lines overnight, mostly
+  soringp1/pnp landings and 3 lock-busy lines.
+- Landing finally occurred **2026-11-24T08:42:33Z**.
+
+Crucially, `run.log` names this candidate **once** — the commission line — and
+never again between approval and landing. The DNS outage explains the
+17:44–19:03Z hour, but not the ~5 h before it (other deliveries landing) or the
+~13.5 h after it. **The dominant delay is that the approved candidate was not
+selected for landing for ~20 h.** Earlier hypotheses (moving-main rebuild,
+canonical dirty-file wait) remain possible but are *not recorded* — see 3.5.
+
+### 3.4 Causes are heterogeneous; one case is not the rule
+
+The 8.3 h second-worst case (`ikca-summons-cleanup-20261119`, head
+`f2a8feb83b29`) has only **3** log lines in its window (two commissions, one
+landing) and no DNS/lock signal. It looks unlike the 20 h case. Several 1–2 h
+cases have heavy lock-busy counts (up to 8 lines). A single explanation will not
+fit all 19; attribution must be per-attempt.
+
+### 3.5 The key observability defect
+
+A case-insensitive search of the *entire* 871-line `run.log` for
+`rebuild|skipped|waiting|base=|preflight|dirty|edited/untracked|ready queue`
+returns **6 lines, all of which are branch names containing "preflight"** —
+there is **no candidate-level log record of why an approved delivery was not
+landed on a given integrator pass**. The prior note's moving-main and
+canonical-preflight mechanisms are therefore *unfalsifiable from current
+durable state*: absence of a log line is not evidence the mechanism never ran,
+only that the log cannot show it. This unobservability is the strongest single
+argument for P1-style reason codes, and a reason to treat the prior note's
+mechanism guesses as open rather than established.
+
+## 4. Reform ideas (for consideration only; none enacted or implemented)
+
+- **Q1 — refine P1: record post-approval selection reasons.** Add, per attempt,
+  `verdict_seen_at`, `first_eligible_at`, a `land_attempts` counter and a
+  `skip_reason` enum (moving-main rebuild / canonical dirty-file wait /
+  ready-queue ordering / external fetch failure / lock contention), written by
+  the integrator at the point it declines to land an approved candidate. This
+  changes only *how the existing gate is observed*; it does not alter approval,
+  independence or exact-commit binding. **Falsifier:** if the reason codes do
+  not account for > half of post-approval wall time over one 7-day window, drop
+  the idea. The 20 h case already demonstrates that the current log cannot
+  account for it at all.
+- **Q2 — test approved-first landing order.** Hypothesis: the tail is partly
+  caused by commissioning new reviews while already-approved candidates wait.
+  Compare a bounded window where the integrator lands all approved candidates
+  before commissioning new reviews against the baseline, measuring approved
+  candidate p90 post-approval, review-work p90 (must not regress), and count of
+  approved-but-unlanded candidates. This is a scheduling experiment on the
+  existing integrator, not a new service or a weaker gate. **Falsifier:** no
+  material p90 reduction, or a review-work regression.
+- **Q3 — keep P2 (hot-ledger append discipline) as a separate measured test.**
+  The prior note's §3.3 association is not contradicted, but no preflight-wait
+  evidence was found here; do not turn the association into a rule without the
+  test.
+- **Q4 — status quo (baseline).** Only 19/157 approved landings exceed an hour
+  and review work is fast. If Q1/Q2 cannot be made cheap and safe, keep the gate
+  intact and the rare tail as the cost of integrity.
+
+**Explicitly rejected:** auto-merging `main` into deliveries, bypassing or
+deferring review, or lowering the Act 21 quality gate to "speed up" landing.
+Those trade auditable integrity for latency and are reliability regressions.
+
+## 5. Free Software / baseline comparison
+
+All measurement uses `git`, POSIX shell, Python standard library and the three
+state files already on `main`. No message bus, scheduler, database or new
+platform is proposed. Q1/Q2, if adopted, should extend the existing integrator
+and its Antonina reviewer path, not add a subsystem. This is the lowest-cost
+baseline; an external observability stack would require a demonstrated failure
+of in-stack reason codes first.
+
+## 6. Falsifiers and limitations
+
+- The join uses `reviews.jsonl`'s `at` as approval time; verdicts recorded only
+  through another path are missed and fall out of the sample entirely.
+- `n = 157` is a later snapshot than the prior note's 133; a quieter week or a
+  reviewer outage would move every figure. Re-run before any decision.
+- Log signals are coarse: `commissioned_review` and `landed` show *other* work
+  proceeding, not that the specific candidate was deliberately skipped; the
+  selection cause remains unobserved (§3.5).
+- The window is heavy with productive-mission landings (soringp1, pnp). The
+  hot/cold association in the prior note is not re-tested here.
+- 19 cases are too few for a per-office or per-path breakdown; §3.4 shows the
+  causes differ. Association is not causation throughout.
+- This is research evidence, not an implementation. No ballot, law,
+  implementation, activation or verification claim is made.
+
+## 7. Questions to the Legislature (dialogue, not a bill)
+
+1. Given that an approved candidate waited 20 h while ~40 other deliveries
+   landed and the log cannot say why, does Parliament want **Q1** (per-attempt
+   post-approval reason codes with a one-window falsifier) before any scheduling
+   change?
+2. Is **Q2** (approved-first landing order, measured against the review-work
+   baseline) an acceptable bounded Executive experiment under existing
+   authority, or does it need a rule?
+3. Should the prior note's P2 remain only a measured hypothesis, given no
+   preflight-wait evidence here?
+
+A one-line disposition on each is enough. No bill is requested or sponsored.
